---
title: 3D Printing
description: Parametric models I designed in OpenSCAD, with a customizer that builds an STL to your dimensions in your browser.
permalink: /3d-printing/
layout: default
---

<%
  models = collections.models.resources.sort_by { |resource| resource.data.date || Time.at(0) }.reverse
%>

<h1>3D Printing</h1>

<div class="model-intro">
  <p>
    Everything here is a part I needed, designed in
    <a href="https://openscad.org">OpenSCAD</a> and printed on my own machine.
    They are written as parametric models rather than fixed shapes, because the
    useful version of a part is almost always the one sized to the thing in
    front of you — your cable, your hose, your wall.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the models are here as models, not just as downloads. Open one, set the
    dimensions, and OpenSCAD will build the STL right in your browser. Nothing
    is uploaded and nothing is stored: your settings live in the page's address,
    which means a link is all it takes to hand someone the exact part you made.
  </p>
</div>

<ul class="model-grid">
  <% models.each do |model| %>
    <%
      scad = model.data.scad
      next if scad.nil?
      count = scad["groups"].sum { |group| group["parameters"].length }
    %>
    <li>
      <a href="<%= model.relative_url %>">
        <article>
          <% if model.data.image %>
            <div class="model-thumb">
              <img src="<%= model.data.image %>" alt="Rendering of the <%= model.data.title %>" loading="lazy">
            </div>
          <% end %>
          <h2><%= model.data.title %></h2>
          <p class="model-blurb"><%= scad["summary"].to_s.tr("\n", " ").split(". ").first %>.</p>
          <p class="model-tags">
            <span class="model-tag"><%= count %> parameters</span>
            <% if scad["presets"] && scad["presets"].any? %>
              <span class="model-tag"><%= scad["presets"].length %> presets</span>
            <% end %>
          </p>
        </article>
      </a>
    </li>
  <% end %>
</ul>

<div class="model-colophon">
  <h2>How this works</h2>
  <p>
    OpenSCAD itself is compiled to WebAssembly and runs on your machine, so
    what you download is built by the same geometry kernel I use on the desktop
    — not an approximation of it. The parameters, their ranges and the
    descriptions under each control are read straight out of the
    <code>.scad</code> files at build time, using OpenSCAD's own Customizer
    annotations. There is one source of truth per model, and it is the model.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first model you open downloads about 11&nbsp;MB of OpenSCAD and library
    code. After that it is cached, and renders take somewhere between a tenth of
    a second and a second and a half.
  </p>
</div>
